insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('fighting-the-system-for-someone-you-love', 'Fighting the System for Someone You Love', 'Forty minutes on hold while the care someone needs sits behind it. Where to aim the anger, why writing everything down changes how you are answered, and how to last the distance.', 'You have been on hold for forty minutes. The form was sent and nobody has it. The person who promised to call back has not. And the care that somebody you love actually needs is sitting behind all of it.

The anger you feel is not a problem with you. It is the correct response to being obstructed while someone you love goes without. The question is only what to do with it so that it works for you rather than against you.

Know what the anger is for

Anger exists to get obstacles moved. That is useful here, because you are facing an obstacle and nobody else is going to move it.

What it is not good at is choosing the target. Spent on the receptionist, the nurse on the phone, or the clerk who answered, it tends to close the door you need open, because most of the people you reach have very little power and plenty of ways to make your day longer.

So aim it at the process and keep it off the person in front of you. Cold, specific and relentless beats hot every time.

Write everything down

This is the single most effective habit, and it is the one most people start too late.

Keep one notebook or one file. Every call: the date, the time, who you spoke to, what they said, what they promised, and by when. Every letter and email in one folder.

Then use it out loud. On the fourteenth of this month, Jane told me the referral was sent. Can you tell me where it is. A record turns a disagreement about what happened into a fact, and it changes how you are answered.

Ask for things in a form they cannot lose

Verbal answers evaporate. Written answers get acted on.

So after a call, email a short summary: thank you for your time, my understanding is that you will do X by Y, please correct me if that is wrong. Ask for decisions in writing, with the reason. Ask who is responsible by name and what their timeline is.

Ask the two questions that unlock most things: what is the next step, and what is this called. Knowing the name of the form, the team or the process is often what lets you ask for it properly.

Find the lever rather than pushing harder

Repeating the same request to the same person is where most of the exhaustion goes.

So go sideways. Ask who makes this decision. Ask for a supervisor politely and specifically. Ask whether there is a complaints process and what the timescale is. Ask about an appeal and the deadline for it, and mind that deadline, because missing it costs more than any argument.

Then find the people whose job this is. A patient advocate, a case manager, a social worker, a charity that works on this exact condition, a benefits adviser, your elected representative''s office. They know the names of things, and they have made this journey before.

A single clear letter to the right office tends to do more than ten calls to the wrong one.

Protect your own supply

This can go on for months, and the version of you that is doing it in month six matters.

So set hours. Make the calls between two and four, not at ten at night, and not in the gaps of a working day where they eat everything. Have somebody else take a shift if there is anyone at all. Accept slower progress in exchange for still being standing.

Watch for what carrying this does to your body and your sleep. Long fights are physically wearing, and people routinely arrive at the end of one in worse shape than the person they were fighting for.

And say it plainly to someone. Not the detail, which nobody can follow, but the weight. I am so angry and so tired, and I cannot stop.

Hold on to what is actually yours

The hardest part is that you cannot guarantee the outcome. You can be organized, persistent and right, and still be refused by a system that has its own reasons.

So measure yourself by what is in your hands. You asked. You wrote it down. You appealed. You found the advocate. You did not let it go quietly.

That is the whole job, and it is a great deal. Whatever the answer turns out to be, the person you love had someone making noise for them, which is more than many people get.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if this fight has left you not sleeping, permanently on edge, or low for weeks, because the toll of advocating for someone is a real one and it is worth naming in an appointment rather than absorbing. A therapist can help with the anger when it starts coming out at home, or at the people who did not deserve it, which is common and does not make you a bad person. For the fight itself, a patient advocate, a case manager, a social worker or a charity that works in this area is practical help rather than sympathy, and asking for one of them earlier rather than later is the thing most people in your position say they wish they had done.', array['Anger', 'Caregiving']::text[], 0, '2026-10-30T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-your-adult-child-moves-back-home', 'When Your Adult Child Moves Back Home', 'Everyone said yes warmly and nobody agreed the rules. What to settle in the first weeks, how to talk about how long, and why mixed feelings about it are normal rather than a failure.', 'They are twenty-six, the lease ended or the relationship did or the job did, and their boxes are in the hall. Everyone said yes quickly and warmly, and now you are three weeks in and nobody is quite sure what the rules are.

You love them and you want to help. You are also aware of a feeling you do not much like, somewhere between crowded and resentful, and you are not sure you are allowed to have it.

Say out loud that this is a change for everybody

The usual mistake is treating it as a non-event. Of course you can stay, it is your home too. Kind, and it skips the part where a household rearranges itself around a new adult.

Both sides lose something real. You lose the quiet, the ease of a house with fewer people in it, and the particular freedom of a late middle age. They lose independence, privacy and the sense of being a full adult, which is often more painful than they let on.

Naming that in one conversation does more than any rule you agree afterward. This is good and it is also an adjustment for all of us.

Agree the practical things early, while everyone is still polite

Three weeks in is the right time. Six months in, after a slow build of unspoken irritation, is much harder.

Money. Whether they pay anything, how much, and starting when. Something is usually better than nothing, even a small amount, because it makes them a contributing adult rather than a guest.

Chores. Not helping out, which means nothing, but specific jobs that are theirs: the trash, the kitchen on these days, their own laundry, the shopping once a week.

Space and noise. Which rooms are shared, what happens after eleven, whether a partner stays over and how often, whether you knock.

Food. Who buys it, who cooks, and whether meals are together or separate. This is the one that generates the most daily friction and the one people are most embarrassed to raise.

Write it down, lightly. Not a contract, just an agreed list, because memory is partial and shared memory is worse.

Talk about how long, even if the answer is uncertain

Nobody wants to ask, and not asking is what turns a stay into an impasse.

You do not need a date. You need a direction and a review. Let us talk again at the end of February and see where things are. What would need to be true for the next step to be possible.

If their plan depends on saving money, say what that looks like in practice, because a vague intention to save is not a plan and both of you will be disappointed.

Treat them as an adult, which is harder than it sounds

The house remembers them at fifteen, and so do you, and old roles reassemble themselves within days.

So notice the pull: asking where they are going, commenting on their hours, their diet, their job search, tidying their room, waiting up. Almost all of it is affection, and it all lands as a demotion.

The useful rule is the one you would use with any adult housemate you were fond of. You would not ask what time they would be home. You would ask them to text if they were not coming for dinner you had cooked.

They have their own work to do here too: being in the house they grew up in while feeling like a failure is a particular kind of hard, and withdrawal to a bedroom is usually that rather than rudeness.

Protect the relationship as well as the arrangement

Living together is not the same as being close, and the first can quietly eat the second.

So do something together that is not logistics. A meal out, a walk, a show you both watch. It stops every exchange being about the dishwasher.

And keep your own life running. Your plans, your friends, the room you use for your things. A parent who dissolves into a service is not a better parent, and the resentment that follows is corrosive and hard to admit to.

If there is a partner in the house, protect that too. Two of you being on the same page before a conversation prevents most of the ways this goes wrong.

Expect the feelings to be mixed

You can be glad they are safe and tired of them being here. You can be proud of them and frustrated at the pace. Both at once, every day, is normal rather than a sign that you are failing at this.

Resentment is information, not a verdict. It usually means a specific thing has been left unsaid for too long, and it shrinks quickly once the specific thing is said.

When to get help

Please talk to a family therapist if the arrangement has become a standoff, if conversations about it now turn into the same argument, or if you suspect something harder underneath the move, such as depression, a drinking problem, debt, or a relationship they have not told you about. A few sessions can get a conversation unstuck faster than another year of trying to have it over dinner. And see your own doctor if this has flattened your mood or your sleep, or if you are carrying a worry about your child that you cannot put down, because that is worth proper attention rather than quietly managing it.', array['Family', 'Life Transitions']::text[], 0, '2026-10-30T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-nothing-structures-your-day', 'When Nothing Structures Your Day', 'Nobody expects you anywhere and the day keeps disappearing. Why structure is not the same as discipline, the three anchors that hold up a day, and how to tell when it has gone further.', 'You wake up and the day is a blank. Nobody expects you anywhere, nothing starts at a particular time, and the whole thing is yours to shape. People with full schedules dream about this. Living it is a different experience.

By mid-afternoon you have done very little, you have been on your phone a great deal, and the day has a used-up feeling without anything to show for it. This happens to almost everyone whose structure disappears, whether through losing a job, retiring, being signed off sick, working freelance or finishing a long project.

Structure is not discipline

It helps to be clear on what is actually missing, because the usual explanation is that you have become lazy, and that explanation is both wrong and useless.

A normal day is held up by scaffolding that other people built. A start time. A commute. Somebody noticing whether you are there. Meetings that chop the day into pieces. A reason to eat lunch at a particular hour.

Take that away and the day has no edges, and a day with no edges is remarkably hard to use. That is not a flaw in your character. It is what happens to nearly everybody.

Which means the work is not to find more willpower. It is to put a little scaffolding back.

Anchor three points, not a timetable

The instinct is to write a full schedule for a perfect day. Those collapse by Wednesday and the collapse feels like proof of something.

So pick three fixed points instead and let the rest be loose.

A get-up time, the same every day, including weekends. This is the most important one, because everything downstream drifts if the start does.

One thing out of the house, at a set time. A walk, a shop, a swim, a coffee. It does not matter what it is, it matters that it is at ten o''clock.

One meal at a fixed hour, eaten at a table.

Three points will hold up a day. A thirty-line plan will not.

Use the first hour deliberately

How the first hour goes tends to decide the day, and the phone is very good at taking it.

So get dressed, in actual clothes, before anything else. People describe this as the thing that separates a day they used from one they lost.

Then do one small useful thing before you look at a screen. Make the bed, wash the dishes, step outside for five minutes. You want one piece of evidence that the day has started.

Keep the phone out of the first hour if you possibly can. An hour of scrolling before you are properly awake is the most reliable way to arrive at noon feeling behind.

Make the day visible

Without meetings, there is nothing to show that time passed or that you did anything, and that absence is what makes the day feel wasted even when it was not.

So write down three things in the morning, small and specific. One phone call, one errand, one twenty-minute task. Cross them off. It is almost childishly simple and it works, because it gives the day a shape you can see.

Keep a done list as well as a to-do list. On days with no external feedback, a line of things you actually did is the only evidence available, and it is worth more than it sounds.

Put other people in it on purpose

An unstructured day is usually also a solitary one, and the loneliness tends to get blamed on the lack of productivity.

So build in one human contact a day, even small. A phone call, a conversation at the counter, a walk with someone, a class, a library, a volunteer shift, a regular group. Something that expects you.

Being expected somewhere is the strongest structure there is, and it is the one most worth arranging if your days have lost it.

Decide when the day ends

Days without a finish bleed into the evening, which is why people in this position often cannot rest. Nothing was finished, so nothing is earned.

So choose a stopping time and stop properly. Shut the laptop, change clothes, go out for ten minutes, cook something. A clear end gives you an evening, and an evening makes tomorrow easier to start.

And allow a slow day without turning it into a verdict. One flat day is a flat day. It is only a pattern if you decide it is.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if the shapeless days come with a mood that has been low for a couple of weeks, if you are sleeping far more or far less than usual, if nothing holds your interest, or if getting up has become genuinely hard rather than just unappealing, because that pattern is common and it is treatable. A therapist can help if this follows a loss, a job ending or an illness, since the empty day is often grief wearing practical clothes. And if your days lost their structure because of health problems, say so plainly at your appointment, because help with pacing and routine is a real part of recovery rather than an afterthought.', array['Habits', 'Motivation']::text[], 0, '2026-10-30T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-you-keep-buying-things-to-feel-better', 'When You Keep Buying Things to Feel Better', 'The lift lasts four minutes and the feeling is still there. What the buying is actually doing, where to put friction so willpower is not the plan, and why shame is fuel rather than brakes.', 'The parcel arrives and there is a small lift for about four minutes. Then it is a thing in a bag in the hall, and the feeling it was supposed to fix is exactly where it was.

You know the pattern. You have promised yourself you will stop. And yet the half hour before bed, or the bad afternoon, or the argument that went nowhere, tends to end with a basket and a confirmation email.

What the buying is actually doing

This is not greed and it is not stupidity, and treating it as either is why the promising-to-stop approach keeps failing.

Buying something does three things fast. It gives you a decision you control at a moment when very little feels controllable. It gives you a small hit of anticipation, which is a genuine lift. And it gives you a few minutes of being someone who is sorting their life out.

Look at the sequence and it is almost always the same: a feeling arrives, the feeling is uncomfortable, the buying interrupts it. That is the mechanism, and it works, briefly, which is exactly why it repeats.

Find out which feeling it answers

The useful question is not why am I like this. It is what was happening ten minutes before.

For most people it is one of a small number of things. Tired. Bored on a long empty evening. Lonely. Flattened after a bad day at work. Angry with somebody and with nowhere to put it. Or ashamed of how your home or your body or your life looks, in which case the thing you buy is a promise that it will be different.

Notice it for a week without changing anything. Just the time, the feeling, and what you bought. That record on its own usually tells you more than any advice.

Put friction where the decision happens

Willpower at the moment of buying is the weakest point in the chain, so do not fight there. Make the moment slower instead.

Delete the apps from your phone and use a browser, which adds steps. Take the saved cards off, so each purchase needs the physical card. Turn off the emails and the notifications that create a reason to look. Unfollow the accounts that make you want things.

Then the rule that does the most work: put it in the basket and leave it for a day. Not never, just not now. A surprising amount of it does not survive the night, and what does survive you can buy without the shame.

Give the feeling somewhere else to go

Taking the behavior away and leaving the feeling in place rarely holds, because the feeling was the reason.

So have two or three things ready for the usual hour. A shower. A walk around the block. A phone call to a particular person. Ten minutes of something with your hands. Getting into bed with a book at nine because you are tired and tired was the whole problem.

None of these is as quick as buying something, which is the honest difficulty. They last longer, which is the honest benefit.

Deal with the money plainly

Shame keeps this in the dark, and in the dark it grows.

So look at the number. Three months of statements, totalled. It will be uncomfortable and it is better than not knowing, because a vague sense of overspending produces anxiety, and a figure produces decisions.

If there is debt, a free debt adviser is the right call, and it is a practical conversation rather than a telling-off. If there is not, set a monthly amount that is yours to spend on wanting things, with no justification required, and keep it separate. Permitted spending tends to shrink. Forbidden spending tends to burst.

Deal with the unopened bags too. Many people have a cupboard of things still in packaging, and opening it or returning what can be returned does a surprising amount for how you feel.

Go gently with yourself about it

Almost everybody has some version of this, and the ones who get on top of it are not the ones who are hardest on themselves.

Shame is actually part of the loop. You feel bad about last week''s spending, feeling bad is uncomfortable, and the thing that reliably interrupts discomfort is buying something. Self-criticism is fuel here, not brakes.

So treat a slip as information about what the hard hour is, and keep the record going. Progress looks like fewer of them, not none.

When to get help

Please talk to a therapist if the buying is something you hide, if it is causing debt or arguments at home, or if you have tried to stop more than once and could not, because compulsive spending responds well to treatment and it is treated as a real thing rather than a weakness. Mention it to your doctor too if it arrived alongside a low or unusually high mood, a new medication, or a stretch of not sleeping, since that pattern is worth looking at properly. For the money itself, a free debt adviser is the most useful professional you can call, and sooner is considerably easier than later.', array['Shame', 'Emotion Regulation']::text[], 0, '2026-10-30T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('doing-something-for-someone-else-when-you-feel-low', 'Doing Something for Someone Else When You Feel Low', 'Irritating advice with something real underneath it. Why helping shifts low mood, how to keep it small enough to finish, and the version of helping that makes everything worse.', 'When you are low, the advice to do something for somebody else can be irritating. It can sound like you are being told that your problem is not thinking enough about others, which is both unkind and not true.

But there is something real underneath it, and it is worth separating from the way it is usually said. Doing something for another person tends to shift how you feel, and it does so for reasons that have nothing to do with being a better person.

Why it works, when it works

Low mood is inward-facing by nature. Attention turns onto yourself, onto what is wrong, onto how you are doing, and it loops there. That looping is a large part of what makes it painful.

Helping someone interrupts that for a while, because the task is outside you and somebody else''s need is concrete in a way your own feelings are not.

It also answers a specific thing that low mood tells you, which is that you make no difference. One small useful act is a piece of direct evidence against that, and evidence works better than reassurance.

And it usually puts you near a person, which matters, because the low weeks tend to be the solitary ones.

Keep it small and keep it specific

This goes wrong when it becomes a project. You decide to be a better friend, or to volunteer every week, and the size of it means it never starts.

So aim much smaller. Send the message you have been meaning to send. Take something round to the neighbor who is unwell. Make a second portion and drop it off. Write the recommendation you promised. Give someone a lift. Put the trash cans back for the house that forgets.

Fifteen minutes is enough. The point is not scale, it is that something real happened for somebody.

Choose the thing you can finish

Low mood makes open-ended tasks very hard, so pick things with an end.

Something that takes one go, that does not need you to be charming, and that nobody will be let down by if you cannot do it next week. Practical beats emotional here: a lift, a meal, a job done, a form filled in, is much easier than an hour of being someone''s support.

If being around people is beyond you this week, there are versions that do not need that. Reviews for a small business you like. Sorting a bag for a donation bin. Donating blood. Writing to somebody who would be glad to hear from you.

Expect it to help afterward rather than during

Nobody should expect to feel a warm glow halfway through, and expecting one is how people conclude this does not work for them.

Often during it you feel fine at best, or still flat. The difference shows up later: a slightly easier evening, one fewer loop, the sense of having been a person who did something today.

That is a modest return and it is reliable. It is not a treatment for depression, and it is one of the few things available that is small, free, and does not require you to feel better first.

Watch the version that costs you

There is a kind of helping that makes everything worse, and people who are already struggling are especially prone to it.

If you are helping to be allowed to exist, or to earn your place, or because you cannot say no, that is not what this is. That version drains you and quietly builds resentment.

The test is simple enough. Does this leave me a little better, or emptier. And can I stop when I need to.

If you are already the person everyone leans on, the right answer this week may be to do nothing for anybody and let somebody do something for you. Being helped is harder for most people than helping, and it is often the thing that was actually missing.

Let it be selfish, which is allowed

You do not have to do this out of pure motives. You can do it because it makes your Tuesday more bearable, and the person on the other end still gets a meal, a lift, or a message that made their week.

Almost nobody is helped by somebody with flawless motivation. They are helped by somebody who turned up.

So pick one thing this week. One message, one errand, one favor, done and finished. Then notice what the evening is like. That is the whole experiment, and it costs very little to run.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if you have been low for more than a couple of weeks, if you are not sleeping or eating properly, or if nothing is holding your interest, because small acts like these sit alongside treatment rather than replacing it, and there is good help available. A therapist can be worth it if you notice that your usefulness to other people has become the main way you value yourself, since that pattern is a heavy one to carry and it tends to go back a long way. And if you are the person who looks after everybody and nobody looks after you, say that out loud to a professional, because it is a reason to get support rather than something to keep managing alone.', array['Values', 'Loneliness']::text[], 0, '2026-10-30T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
on conflict (slug) do update set
  title        = excluded.title,
  excerpt      = excluded.excerpt,
  body_md      = excluded.body_md,
  tags         = excluded.tags,
  min_level    = 0,
  published_at = excluded.published_at;
